## 39. Intersection Formula

If \(n\) straight lines are drawn such that:

- no two are parallel
- no three pass through the same point

then maximum intersections:

```text
\boxed{{n\choose2}}
```

or:

```text
\boxed{\frac{n(n-1)}2}
```

---

## 40. Example: 4 Lines

Maximum intersections:

```text
{4\choose2}=6
```

---

## 41. Example: 5 Lines

```text
{5\choose2}=10
```

This is the maximum only under the required assumptions.

---

## 42. Maximum Regions from Lines

If \(n\) lines are drawn in general position, the maximum number of regions is:

```text
\boxed{
\frac{n(n+1)}2+1
}
```

Examples:

| Lines | Max regions |
|---:|---:|
| 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 2 |
| 2 | 4 |
| 3 | 7 |
| 4 | 11 |
| 5 | 16 |
| 6 | 22 |

This is useful for advanced figure-counting questions.

---

## 43. Why Counting Gets Difficult

A diagram can contain:

```text
small figures
     +
combined figures
     +
overlapping figures
     +
rotated figures
     +
hidden figures
```

Therefore:

```text
\boxed{
Total\neq Visible\ Small\ Figures
}
```

---

## 44. The Layer Method

Imagine the diagram has layers:

```text
Layer 1 → smallest
Layer 2 → combinations
Layer 3 → larger
Layer 4 → complete figure
```

Count each layer separately.

---

## 45. Layer Diagram

```text
              OUTER FIGURE
          ┌─────────────────┐
          │   LARGE         │
          │   FIGURES       │
          │  ┌───────────┐  │
          │  │ MEDIUM    │  │
          │  │ ┌───────┐ │  │
          │  │ │SMALL  │ │  │
          │  │ └───────┘ │  │
          │  └───────────┘  │
          └─────────────────┘
```

---

## 46. The Sweep Method

For difficult diagrams, mentally scan:

```text
Top → Bottom
Left → Right
```

Then repeat by size.

Example:

```text
PASS 1 → small
PASS 2 → medium
PASS 3 → large
PASS 4 → composite
```

---

## 47. Marking Method

On paper, label figures temporarily:

```text
T1
T2
T3
T4
```

Then mark:

```text
✓ counted
```

This prevents double counting.

---

## 48. Bounding-Box Method

For each candidate figure, identify its boundary.

```text
┌─────────┐
│ figure  │
└─────────┘
```

Ask:

> Are all required boundary lines actually present?

If even one boundary is missing, it is not a valid figure.

---

## 49. Boundary Validation

A shape counts only if:

```text
Boundary 1 ✓
Boundary 2 ✓
Boundary 3 ✓
Boundary 4 ✓
Closed ✓
```

Otherwise:

```text
NOT A FIGURE
```

---

## 50. Open vs Closed Shapes

Example:

```text
┌─────┐
│     │
└─────
```

This is not a closed rectangle.

A valid rectangle needs all four sides.

---

## 51. Hidden Boundary Trap

Sometimes a boundary is shared by another figure.

Do not ignore shared lines.

A line can be part of:

```text
small figure
AND
large figure
```

at the same time.

---

## 52. Shared Line Principle

```text
┌───┬───┐
│ A │ B │
└───┴───┘
```

The middle line belongs to both small rectangles and may also help create the outer rectangle.

One line can participate in multiple figures.

---

## 53. Double Counting Trap

If you count:

```text
A
B
A+B
```

then A and B are separate figures and A+B is another figure.

Do not count A+B twice through different descriptions.

---

## 54. Unique Boundary Principle

Two candidate figures are the same if they have the same complete boundary, even if you discovered them through different routes.

This is crucial in complex diagrams.

---

## 55. Counting by Boundary Sets

Conceptually:

```text
F=\{e_1,e_2,\ldots,e_k\}
```

where \(e_i\) are boundary edges.

Two paths that produce the same edge set represent the same figure.

---
